== Vision-Language Models for Dense Prediction and Segmentation

Large vision-language models have rapidly acquired the spatial reasoning capabilities needed for dense visual understanding. Liu et al. @liu2024llava introduced LLaVA, which pairs a CLIP visual encoder with a large language model through a simple projection layer, establishing _visual instruction tuning_ as a scalable recipe for multimodal reasoning. Subsequent architectures extended this foundation with finer spatial awareness. You et al. @you2024ferret proposed Ferret, a hybrid region representation that enables referring and grounding at arbitrary granularity by combining discrete and continuous spatial features. Peng et al. @peng2024kosmos2 demonstrated with Kosmos-2 that spatial coordinates can be expressed as discrete location tokens within the language modeling vocabulary, enabling _grounded text generation_ without auxiliary detection heads. Chen et al. @chen2024spatialvlm further endowed VLMs with quantitative spatial reasoning, training models to estimate metric distances, object sizes, and spatial relations from single images. Together, these advances show that modern VLMs possess increasingly precise spatial understanding, a prerequisite for any system that must reason about _where_ to place segmentation prompts.

A parallel line of work has sought to connect VLMs directly to pixel-level segmentation decoders. Lai et al. @lai2024lisa introduced LISA, a _reasoning segmentation_ framework in which the language model emits a special #text[\<SEG\>] token whose embedding is projected into SAM's prompt space, enabling end-to-end segmentation from complex linguistic queries. Yang et al. @yang2023setofmark offered a complementary, training-free alternative with Set-of-Mark prompting, which overlays numbered visual markers on SAM-generated region proposals so that a frozen VLM can ground its answers by referring to specific marks. Ren et al. @ren2024pixellm proposed PixelLM for multi-target pixel reasoning, decoding multiple segmentation masks from a single complex query through a dedicated pixel decoder. At larger scale, Rasheed et al. @rasheed2024glamm presented GLaMM, a grounded conversational model that couples a multimodal LLM with a SAM-based pixel decoder trained on 7.5 million concepts and 810 million region annotations. Zhang et al. @zhang2024psalm unified referring, interactive, and semantic segmentation within a single language-model framework by attaching a Mask2Former decoder, while Zhang et al. @zhang2024llavagrounding connected LLaVA to a grounding-aware segmentation decoder for visually grounded dialogue. LISA is the most directly relevant of these systems, as it demonstrates that LLM reasoning can be channeled into SAM's prompt interface.

Our V3 system departs from all of the above in a fundamental respect. Rather than using a VLM as a _single-pass_ prompt generator that produces one segmentation per query, we deploy Qwen-VL @bai2025qwen25vl as an _interactive segmentation policy_ that reasons about prompt placement across multiple turns. At each step the model observes the current image with its overlay of prior prompts, performs chain-of-thought spatial reasoning, and emits the next click coordinate. This iterative, closed-loop formulation enables deliberate correction of earlier errors, a capability absent from end-to-end architectures such as LISA and GLaMM. The resulting system achieves 0.853 Dice with an average of only 1.4 clicks, dramatically outperforming both the single-pass VLM segmentation paradigm and our own RL-trained policies.
